{
  "result": "The city kept its lost umbrellas in a room beneath the train station. Every Thursday, Mara went down with a pencil and a thermos to catalogue the new arrivals. She was supposed to note their color and condition. Instead, she wrote down what she guessed they had been through lately.\n\nOne umbrella, yellow with a bent tip, had clearly been used to point out a third-floor window to someone who refused to look up. A black one smelled faintly of oranges. Mara gave that one three possible histories, then crossed out the saddest. There was no need to be thorough.\n\nLate one afternoon a man asked if anyone had turned in a green umbrella with a wooden duck for a handle. Mara found it at once. In her notebook she had written: *Bought as a joke, kept for twenty years.* The man took it and tested the stiff little catch.\n\n\u201cNot mine,\u201d he said. \u201cMine had a goose.\u201d He set it back so carefully that Mara wondered if he had come all this way hoping to be wrong. She asked whether he wanted to leave his number. He thought about it, then shook his head. \u201cIt was my father\u2019s, actually.\u201d\n\nAfter he left, Mara made a card for the goose umbrella, though it wasn\u2019t there. She left the spaces for color and condition blank. Then she put it in the drawer with the others, where it would be ready in case someone came downstairs carrying a goose by the neck.",
